Third-party code

Nothing is vendored into this repository: every file here is covered by the
licence above. A linked `jr` binary is not, and a distributed binary has to
carry these licences with it. `go.mod` is the authority; this is the same list
spelled out.

    github.com/BurntSushi/toml            MIT
                                          Copyright (c) 2013 TOML authors
    github.com/spf13/cobra                Apache-2.0
    github.com/spf13/pflag                BSD-3-Clause
                                          Copyright (c) 2012 Alex Ogier
                                          Copyright (c) 2012 The Go Authors
    golang.org/x/term                     BSD-3-Clause
                                          Copyright 2009 The Go Authors
    gopkg.in/yaml.v3                      MIT and Apache-2.0
    golang.org/x/sys                      BSD-3-Clause
                                          Copyright 2009 The Go Authors
                                          (indirect, reached through x/term)
    github.com/inconshreveable/mousetrap  Apache-2.0
                                          (indirect, reached through cobra, and
                                          linked only in a Windows build)

Five direct dependencies and two indirect. Each licence text is in the module's
own source, which `go mod download` fetches and `go.sum` pins.

This list is asserted rather than maintained by hand:
`internal/lint/notice_test.go` links every shipped profile, on every platform
this builds for, and fails when a module reaches the binary without appearing
above. It was written because golang.org/x/term did not: it is imported with no
build tag, every profile links it, and its BSD-3-Clause notice was missing from
the one file whose whole job is to travel with the binary.

gopkg.in/yaml.v3 carries a NOTICE of its own. Section 4(d) of the Apache License
requires it to travel with the work, so it is reproduced here in full:

